1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,850
Expression here shows a Human Protein Atlas RNA-sequencing study.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:13,000
The bars compare estimated RNA abundance across the tissues sampled, on the normalised measure named on the vertical axis.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,250
Above the chart are links to the project record and the publication — where the data came from and how the study was done.

4
00:00:20,250 --> 00:00:24,400
That is the part to follow if you intend to rely on the numbers.

5
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:29,175
This is the step people skip, and it is the one that matters for a paper.

6
00:00:29,175 --> 00:00:34,550
An expression figure in a gene record is a summary of somebody's experiment.

7
00:00:34,550 --> 00:00:41,250
The record links back to that experiment: the GEO series, its samples, its platform, its publication.

8
00:00:41,250 --> 00:00:45,900
Two clicks takes you from a bar chart to the study design that produced it.

9
00:00:45,900 --> 00:00:51,600
If you are going to say in writing that a gene is expressed in a tissue, follow that link first.

10
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:55,700
The summary is a convenience; the study is the evidence.

11
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:59,750
A gene record led us to expression data.

12
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:03,625
To find out what the protein does, we go to UniProt.

13
00:01:03,625 --> 00:01:07,450
Three databases there, answering different questions.

14
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:13,350
UniProtKB holds protein sequences and annotations about function and structure.

15
00:01:13,350 --> 00:01:21,800
Its Swiss-Prot section is reviewed by curators; TrEMBL is annotated automatically and has not been through manual review.

16
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:32,125
The review status tells you how the entry was produced — and to judge a particular statement, follow the evidence attached to that statement rather than trusting the entry as a whole.

17
00:01:32,125 --> 00:01:34,500
UniParc is an archive.

18
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:42,425
It stores each distinct sequence once and links it to every record it has appeared in, which is how you trace where a sequence came from.

19
00:01:42,425 --> 00:01:52,350
UniRef groups sequences into clusters at ninety, fifty or a hundred per cent identity — useful when you want a representative set rather than everything.

20
00:01:52,350 --> 00:01:55,425
We want function, so UniProtKB.

21
00:01:55,425 --> 00:02:02,275
UniProt is not one database, and the distinction between its parts decides how much you should trust an entry.

22
00:02:02,275 --> 00:02:09,725
Swiss-Prot is the reviewed section: a curator has read the literature and the entry carries evidence for what it claims.

23
00:02:09,725 --> 00:02:16,350
TrEMBL is the unreviewed section, produced automatically from translated coding sequences.

24
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:27,725
The sizes are not comparable — reviewed entries number in the hundreds of thousands, unreviewed in the hundreds of millions — and that ratio has only gone one way since sequencing became cheap.

25
00:02:27,725 --> 00:02:39,200
UniRef is a different kind of thing again: clustered sequences, at ninety, fifty or a hundred per cent identity, used when you want a non-redundant set rather than every entry.

26
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:45,900
UniParc is the archive, which keeps every sequence ever seen even after the entry it came from has gone.

27
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:49,900
When somebody says "it is in UniProt", ask which part.

28
00:02:49,900 --> 00:02:57,550
It is worth putting numbers on that ratio, because they are the clearest single statement about where molecular biology now is.

29
00:02:57,550 --> 00:03:07,450
The reviewed section holds a few hundred thousand entries and has grown slowly for twenty years, because reading the literature for an entry takes a person a day or more.

30
00:03:07,450 --> 00:03:12,875
The unreviewed section holds a few hundred million and grows with sequencing capacity.

31
00:03:12,875 --> 00:03:18,125
So the reviewed fraction of UniProt is well under one per cent, and falling.

32
00:03:18,125 --> 00:03:22,650
That is not a criticism of the curators; it is arithmetic.

33
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:31,475
What follows from it is a habit: when an annotation matters to your conclusion, check whether a person ever looked at it, because the odds are against it.

34
00:03:32,075 --> 00:03:38,500
Select UniProtKB beside the search box and enter the same words we used at NCBI.

35
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:42,750
The collection you select decides what kind of record comes back.

36
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:48,850
Here each row is a protein entry, with fields like protein name and organism to choose by.

37
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:58,100
Searching within UniProtKB rather than from the front page, for the same reason as at NCBI: one kind of record, one meaning per column.

38
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:04,275
The query contains the word human, and the sidebar still offers zebrafish and mouse.

39
00:04:04,275 --> 00:04:07,250
The word in the query is not a filter.

40
00:04:07,250 --> 00:04:10,625
So select Human under popular organisms.

41
00:04:10,625 --> 00:04:18,775
The status filter chooses reviewed or unreviewed entries, and for this we want one that has been curated by hand, so Reviewed.

42
00:04:18,775 --> 00:04:24,700
Even then several human haemoglobin subunits remain, so we still have to read the names.

43
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:29,725
Two filters do most of the work, and they are on the left of every result page.

44
00:04:29,725 --> 00:04:32,750
Organism narrows to one species.

45
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:35,350
Reviewed narrows to Swiss-Prot.

46
00:04:35,350 --> 00:04:43,625
Applied together they usually take a result list of thousands down to a single entry, and that single entry is the one you want to cite.

47
00:04:43,625 --> 00:04:55,250
This is also the point at which to notice how many unreviewed entries you have just discarded, because that number is a fair measure of how much of what is in the database has never been read by a person.

48
00:04:55,850 --> 00:05:01,650
In the table, look for haemoglobin subunit beta and Homo sapiens on the same row.

49
00:05:01,650 --> 00:05:09,550
The other rows are human haemoglobin subunits too — epsilon, delta — so the organism alone gets you only part of the way.

50
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:17,825
Open the entry and both fields are confirmed in the header, along with "Reviewed", which tells us the curation status of what we are reading.

51
00:05:17,825 --> 00:05:22,975
Check the protein name and the organism, exactly as with the gene record.

52
00:05:22,975 --> 00:05:25,850
Note the two identifiers at the top.

53
00:05:25,850 --> 00:05:32,000
The accession, P68871, is stable: it will still point here in ten years.

54
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:36,350
The entry name, HBB_HUMAN, is readable but can change.

55
00:05:36,350 --> 00:05:39,900
Cite the accession; say the entry name out loud.

56
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:43,925
We came here to find out what beta-globin does.

57
00:05:43,925 --> 00:05:49,325
Under Function, the first annotation describes its role in oxygen transport.

58
00:05:49,325 --> 00:05:56,475
Beside that statement is a label saying one publication, and it leads to a paper on a beta-globin variant.

59
00:05:56,475 --> 00:06:03,275
Follow it and you can see what the authors actually did, and how their result relates to the sentence in the database.

60
00:06:03,275 --> 00:06:10,750
That is the distinction worth taking away: finding a record is not the same as checking the evidence for a claim inside it.

61
00:06:10,750 --> 00:06:20,525
The function section is the reason to come to UniProt rather than anywhere else, and the evidence tags beside each statement are the reason to read it carefully.

62
00:06:20,525 --> 00:06:30,525
Every claim carries a code saying where it came from: inferred from experiment, from a publication, from sequence similarity, or from an automatic annotation.

63
00:06:30,525 --> 00:06:36,725
Two statements sitting in the same paragraph in the same typeface can have entirely different standing.

64
00:06:36,725 --> 00:06:38,950
Hover over the tags.

65
00:06:38,950 --> 00:06:47,425
A function supported by a paper and a function propagated from a similar sequence are both worth knowing, and only one of them is a result.

66
00:06:47,425 --> 00:06:51,625
One more thing about evidence codes before we leave UniProt.

67
00:06:51,625 --> 00:07:00,775
They are hierarchical, and the distinction that matters most is between evidence from the protein itself and evidence transferred from something similar.

68
00:07:00,775 --> 00:07:05,200
"Inferred from direct assay" means somebody measured this protein.

69
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:13,250
"Inferred from sequence similarity" means somebody measured a different protein and the annotation travelled here along an alignment.

70
00:07:13,250 --> 00:07:18,600
Both are legitimate; only the first is a fact about the molecule in front of you.

71
00:07:18,600 --> 00:07:23,575
Whole fields have been built on annotations that turned out to have travelled a long way.

72
00:07:24,175 --> 00:07:26,350
We have searched by name.

73
00:07:26,350 --> 00:07:32,075
Now we use the approved symbol, HBB, to connect records across databases.

74
00:07:32,075 --> 00:07:36,550
HGNC is the HUGO Gene Nomenclature Committee.

75
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:43,150
It approves and maintains one standard symbol and one descriptive name for each human gene it names.

76
00:07:43,150 --> 00:07:50,550
That shared vocabulary is what lets researchers refer to the same gene in a paper and find it again in a database.

77
00:07:50,550 --> 00:07:58,300
The report shows the symbol HBB, the approved name haemoglobin subunit beta, and a nomenclature identifier.

78
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:03,975
That identifier stays stable even if the approved name or symbol later changes.

79
00:08:03,975 --> 00:08:07,525
Now the same starting point from the other direction.

80
00:08:07,525 --> 00:08:14,575
The official symbol is agreed — HBB — and what differs is what each resource calls the thing that symbol names.

81
00:08:15,175 --> 00:08:19,625
This is the NCBI Gene record we reached earlier by name.

82
00:08:19,625 --> 00:08:24,400
The number highlighted is its identifier within NCBI Gene.

83
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:31,300
The summary credits HGNC as the source of the approved symbol and links back to the nomenclature record.

84
00:08:31,300 --> 00:08:33,800
It also links to Ensembl.

85
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:41,650
Those cross-references are how you confirm that two resources are describing the same gene while each uses its own identifier.

86
00:08:41,650 --> 00:08:48,525
At NCBI the symbol leads to a Gene record with a numeric identifier, 3043.

87
00:08:48,525 --> 00:08:55,425
The number is arbitrary and permanent, which is a good combination: it carries no meaning and it never moves.

88
00:08:56,025 --> 00:09:04,950
Ensembl uses the same symbol for the same gene and gives its record an identifier beginning E-N-S-G, which marks an Ensembl gene.

89
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:08,050
Compare that with the NCBI number.

90
00:09:08,050 --> 00:09:11,925
Both lead to a gene record; neither is more correct.

91
00:09:11,925 --> 00:09:17,275
The description cites the same nomenclature identifier, which ties the two together.

92
00:09:17,275 --> 00:09:25,900
This view also gives the location on chromosome eleven in the GRCh38 assembly, and links to the annotated transcripts.

93
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:32,650
The screenshot is from one particular Ensembl release, so check the live record for current annotation.

94
00:09:32,650 --> 00:09:40,800
At EMBL-EBI, through Ensembl, the same gene is ENSG00000244734.

95
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:49,850
The prefix tells you the species and the kind of object before you look anything up, which is a property the NCBI number deliberately does not have.

96
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:54,950
The last magnifier highlights an accession in the Japanese archive.

97
00:09:54,950 --> 00:10:01,650
Read the header and the definition: this is a submitted human beta-globin messenger RNA sequence.

98
00:10:01,650 --> 00:10:09,325
The accession line gives the accession, and the version line adds a suffix — point one, the first version of that sequence.

99
00:10:09,325 --> 00:10:14,400
The notebook retrieves the same record through NCBI and through ENA.

100
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:19,975
The partners share the submitted sequence and the accession, and only display it differently.

101
00:10:19,975 --> 00:10:26,425
Contrast that with the gene records, where two resources have two different identifiers for one gene.

102
00:10:26,425 --> 00:10:32,800
So before using an identifier, know which resource it belongs to and what kind of record it fetches.

103
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:35,875
Here, the header tells us it is mRNA.

104
00:10:35,875 --> 00:10:49,175
And at DDBJ, as at the other two INSDC partners, the same sequence appears under an accession from the shared space — here V00497.1, one of the oldest records in the archive.

105
00:10:49,175 --> 00:10:52,725
Three resources, three identifiers, one gene.

106
00:10:52,725 --> 00:10:58,175
None of them is wrong, and none of them can be substituted for another in a methods section.

107
00:10:58,775 --> 00:11:05,525
Suppose a colleague asks you to repeat an analysis and says, "I used HBB."  You recognise the gene.

108
00:11:05,525 --> 00:11:08,950
You still do not know what went into the analysis.

109
00:11:08,950 --> 00:11:14,125
The name is a starting point, and the search opens a whole collection of records.

110
00:11:14,125 --> 00:11:22,175
Some describe the human gene in different resources — the nomenclature record, the NCBI record, the Ensembl record.

111
00:11:22,175 --> 00:11:29,375
Others describe particular sequences, transcripts, proteins, variants, structures or families connected with it.

112
00:11:29,375 --> 00:11:36,800
The collection is large because many resources organise different information around the same biological subject.

113
00:11:36,800 --> 00:11:39,525
That feels like chaos at first.

114
00:11:39,525 --> 00:11:44,925
It is the opposite: each identifier is a way to point at exactly one record.

115
00:11:44,925 --> 00:11:47,775
This is the slide to photograph.

116
00:11:47,775 --> 00:12:02,750
One approved symbol in the middle, and around it every identifier that symbol leads to: nomenclature, gene records at two resources, a genomic reference sequence, a clinical variant record, transcripts, proteins, a structure, a domain family.

117
00:12:02,750 --> 00:12:05,075
They are not alternatives.

118
00:12:05,075 --> 00:12:12,375
Each names a different object at a different level, and the lines between them are the cross-references we will follow in a moment.

119
00:12:12,975 --> 00:12:17,775
So two questions have to be answered before the analysis can be repeated.

120
00:12:17,775 --> 00:12:20,125
First, what kind of sequence?

121
00:12:20,125 --> 00:12:25,625
A transcript study needs an mRNA; an amino-acid change needs a protein.

122
00:12:25,625 --> 00:12:32,650
In RefSeq the prefix tells you which — N-M for a messenger RNA record, N-P for its protein product.

123
00:12:32,650 --> 00:12:35,750
UniProt uses a different convention.

124
00:12:35,750 --> 00:12:44,150
One accession identifies the entry, and for a protein with several isoforms a numbered suffix after a dash picks the isoform.

125
00:12:44,150 --> 00:12:50,675
That dash-number means something quite different from RefSeq's version suffix, which is easy to confuse.

126
00:12:50,675 --> 00:13:03,050
Variants can have their own identifiers beginning V-A-R, and processed chains identifiers beginning P-R-O — the mature beta-globin chain, after its initial methionine is removed, has one of those.

127
00:13:03,050 --> 00:13:07,475
Those point at features inside the entry rather than at the entry.

128
00:13:07,475 --> 00:13:18,200
Modifications such as phosphorylation are recorded by type and position, so identifying a modified form takes the accession plus the modification plus where it sits.

129
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:20,800
Second question: which version?

130
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:24,500
Which of them you use is decided by what you are claiming.

131
00:13:24,500 --> 00:13:29,650
A coordinate claim needs the genomic sequence and the assembly it refers to.

132
00:13:29,650 --> 00:13:38,850
A sequence claim needs the transcript accession with its version — the version, because the number without it names a record that has changed over time.

133
00:13:38,850 --> 00:13:42,025
A function claim needs the protein entry.

134
00:13:42,025 --> 00:13:48,825
A structural claim needs the PDB code and, increasingly, whether the structure was measured or predicted.

135
00:13:48,825 --> 00:13:57,100
The common failure is to name the gene symbol and nothing else, which is the one identifier in this picture that names no sequence at all.

136
00:13:57,100 --> 00:14:05,050
There is one more identifier on this picture worth singling out, because it was designed to solve exactly the problem the picture describes.

137
00:14:05,050 --> 00:14:15,300
For most human genes, NCBI and EMBL-EBI have agreed on one transcript — the same sequence, the same coordinates, with a matching accession on each side.

138
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:26,375
That agreed transcript is the one to use when you need a single representative and do not have a reason to prefer another, and it is marked in the record rather than left for you to work out.

139
00:14:26,375 --> 00:14:35,450
Before it existed, two groups analysing the same gene could reasonably choose different transcripts and get different answers about where a variant fell.

140
00:14:35,450 --> 00:14:39,500
That is the kind of problem a shared identifier removes.

141
00:14:40,100 --> 00:14:45,525
Once you know the resource, the first letters and the final characters tell you a great deal.

142
00:14:45,525 --> 00:14:49,875
These are examples; every resource sets its own conventions.

143
00:14:49,875 --> 00:14:54,200
RefSeq: N-M for messenger RNA, N-P for protein.

144
00:14:54,200 --> 00:15:01,025
The number after the dot is the sequence version, and the mRNA and its protein have independent versions.

145
00:15:01,025 --> 00:15:04,075
Keep both the accession and the version.

146
00:15:04,075 --> 00:15:12,350
Ensembl: E-N-S-G for a gene, E-N-S-T for a transcript, E-N-S-P for a protein, with extra letters for other species.

147
00:15:12,350 --> 00:15:23,950
The number after the dot is the version of that object, and Ensembl updates genes, transcripts and proteins by different rules — so a gene version is not a count of changes to its DNA.

148
00:15:23,950 --> 00:15:29,525
In the read archives, three different prefixes mark runs originating at the three partners.

149
00:15:29,525 --> 00:15:37,200
Because the partners exchange records, the prefix tells you where the data were deposited, not the only place you can get them.

150
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:44,225
GEO uses one prefix for a series, which groups the samples of a study, and another for an individual sample.

151
00:15:44,225 --> 00:15:50,650
The patterns in this table are worth memorising, because they let you read an identifier before you look it up.

152
00:15:50,650 --> 00:16:01,375
NM underscore is a curated messenger RNA; NP underscore is the protein it codes for; XM and XP are the same things predicted rather than curated.

153
00:16:01,375 --> 00:16:10,750
The ENS prefixes split the same way — G for gene, T for transcript, P for protein — with the species written in for anything that is not human.

154
00:16:10,750 --> 00:16:18,275
SRR, ERR and DRR are sequencing runs, and the first letter tells you which of the three archives took the submission.

155
00:16:18,275 --> 00:16:23,025
GSE is a GEO series, GSM one sample within it.

156
00:16:23,025 --> 00:16:29,300
A UniProt accession followed by a dash and a number is an isoform, not a separate protein.

157
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:34,300
RF numbers are RNA families, PF numbers protein families.

158
00:16:34,300 --> 00:16:36,725
The point is not the trivia.

159
00:16:36,725 --> 00:16:44,175
It is that an accession in a paper tells you what kind of claim is being made, and a surprising number of papers make the wrong one.

160
00:16:44,175 --> 00:16:48,425
Two warnings about reading patterns, since we are encouraging it.

161
00:16:48,425 --> 00:16:54,225
The first: a prefix tells you what kind of object a record is, not whether it is any good.

162
00:16:54,225 --> 00:17:04,725
An XM accession is a predicted transcript, which may be perfectly correct; an NM accession is curated, which does not make it the right transcript for your question.

163
00:17:04,725 --> 00:17:09,575
The second: versions are part of the identifier and are routinely dropped.

164
00:17:09,575 --> 00:17:13,550
The suffix after the dot changes when the sequence changes.

165
00:17:13,550 --> 00:17:21,925
A paper that cites the accession without it has named a record rather than a sequence, and nobody can reproduce the analysis exactly.

166
00:17:22,525 --> 00:17:30,950
Now our colleague has told us exactly which record they used, and we can follow it outwards instead of starting a new name search at every site.

167
00:17:30,950 --> 00:17:39,325
A cross-reference is a pointer from one record to related information somewhere else, usually shown as a clickable identifier.

168
00:17:39,325 --> 00:17:43,575
Here the source is the UniProtKB beta-globin entry.

169
00:17:43,575 --> 00:17:48,875
One cross-reference leads to the NCBI gene record — protein to gene.

170
00:17:48,875 --> 00:17:52,750
Another leads to the RefSeq reference protein sequence.

171
00:17:52,750 --> 00:18:01,975
A third leads to a Protein Data Bank entry: an experimentally determined haemoglobin complex containing both the alpha and the beta chains.

172
00:18:01,975 --> 00:18:05,675
Notice those are three different kinds of destination.

173
00:18:05,675 --> 00:18:11,225
Read the resource name and the relationship before you interpret where a link has taken you.

174
00:18:11,225 --> 00:18:16,050
And when you find the record you want, keep the full identifier and its version.

175
00:18:16,050 --> 00:18:22,200
A cross-reference is a promise that two records describe the same thing, made by somebody who checked.

176
00:18:22,200 --> 00:18:26,275
That is what makes them worth following rather than searching again.

177
00:18:26,275 --> 00:18:37,775
From the UniProt entry you reach the gene record, the reference protein sequence and the structure, without typing a name into another search box and without the risk that you land on the mouse version.

178
00:18:37,775 --> 00:18:44,500
When you build a dossier on a protein — and that is what the lab after this lecture asks you to do — this is the route.

179
00:18:44,500 --> 00:18:51,225
Start at one well-chosen record and follow its links, rather than starting a new search at every resource.

180
00:18:51,825 --> 00:18:55,575
Identifiers and cross-references gave us the map.

181
00:18:55,575 --> 00:18:58,300
Now the routes, and there are three of them.

182
00:18:58,300 --> 00:19:04,800
The first is the record page itself — search, read the annotations, check the evidence, follow the links out.

183
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:09,250
We have been doing that for the last half hour, so we will not do it again.

184
00:19:09,250 --> 00:19:21,775
The second is programmatic access: a command-line tool or a script asks the database directly, through an interface built for software rather than for people, and gets back something a program can work with.

185
00:19:21,775 --> 00:19:31,375
The third is a genome browser, which draws everything at its position along a reference genome, so DNA, transcripts and proteins can be looked at together.

186
00:19:31,375 --> 00:19:34,325
The rest of this part is about those two.

187
00:19:34,925 --> 00:19:39,025
The simplest version is one command producing one file.

188
00:19:39,025 --> 00:19:43,650
Wget fetches whatever a web address returns and saves it.

189
00:19:43,650 --> 00:19:52,275
The address here identifies the UniProt entry for human haemoglobin beta, and the ending asks for it in FASTA format.

190
00:19:52,275 --> 00:19:55,375
The file arrives named after the entry.

191
00:19:55,375 --> 00:19:57,900
The second command prints it.

192
00:19:57,900 --> 00:20:08,650
The first line begins with a greater-than sign and names the record, the protein and the organism; the amino-acid sequence follows, a hundred and forty-seven residues of it.

193
00:20:08,650 --> 00:20:14,725
The dollar sign at the start of each command is the prompt your terminal shows you — you do not type it.

194
00:20:15,325 --> 00:20:18,700
A file on disk is not always what you want.

195
00:20:18,700 --> 00:20:25,100
Often you want a few fields out of the record, in a form your program can carry straight into the next step.

196
00:20:25,100 --> 00:20:31,500
This asks for the same UniProt entry, but in JSON — structured text with named fields.

197
00:20:31,500 --> 00:20:34,625
Same protein, different representation.

198
00:20:34,625 --> 00:20:39,725
Reading down: import the requests library, build the address, send the request.

199
00:20:39,725 --> 00:20:50,325
The timeout stops the script hanging forever on a server that never answers, and the status check turns a server error into an error you notice, rather than a file full of error page.

200
00:20:50,325 --> 00:20:58,475
Then the response becomes ordinary Python data, and three fields come out of it — accession, organism, and the length of the sequence.

201
00:20:59,075 --> 00:21:04,275
A genome browser adds the one thing a record page cannot give you: position.

202
00:21:04,275 --> 00:21:13,200
Everything is drawn against a reference genome, each horizontal track a different collection of annotations, all sharing one set of coordinates.

203
00:21:13,200 --> 00:21:20,500
This is the human HBB region at UCSC, with the GENCODE and RefSeq annotations switched on.

204
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:27,750
From here you can compare transcript structures, see what sits beside the gene, and follow links back to the records underneath.

205
00:21:27,750 --> 00:21:32,275
Before any of that is useful, you have to be able to read the display.

206
00:21:32,275 --> 00:21:35,225
That is what the next few slides are for.

207
00:21:35,825 --> 00:21:39,550
Start with the header, because it tells you what you are looking at.

208
00:21:39,550 --> 00:21:46,825
The organism is human and the assembly is GRCh38, which UCSC calls hg38.

209
00:21:46,825 --> 00:21:53,775
The date is when that assembly was released; the annotations drawn on top of it get updated far more often.

210
00:21:53,775 --> 00:21:59,975
The interval gives the stretch of chromosome eleven on screen — a little over two thousand bases.

211
00:21:59,975 --> 00:22:05,250
Keep the assembly name together with the coordinates whenever you write a position down.

212
00:22:05,250 --> 00:22:09,000
Coordinates on their own point at nothing in particular.

213
00:22:09,600 --> 00:22:18,800
The ruler gives positions along chromosome eleven, increasing left to right, and the scale bar shows what a thousand bases looks like at this zoom.

214
00:22:18,800 --> 00:22:22,075
Zooming changes how much fits on the screen.

215
00:22:22,075 --> 00:22:25,175
It does not change the sequence underneath.

216
00:22:25,175 --> 00:22:29,750
Below the ruler is the GENCODE label with a release number.

217
00:22:29,750 --> 00:22:38,475
GENCODE is the project that works out where genes are and what transcripts they make, exon boundaries and coding regions included.

218
00:22:38,475 --> 00:22:43,875
Its release number is its own and has nothing to do with the assembly named above it.

219
00:22:43,875 --> 00:22:48,125
And the note saying six items were filtered out is worth reading.

220
00:22:48,125 --> 00:22:52,600
Six transcript annotations in this region are not being drawn.

221
00:22:52,600 --> 00:22:55,000
They are still in the database.

222
00:22:55,000 --> 00:23:00,175
The browser is showing you a chosen subset, and it is useful to know when it is.

223
00:23:00,775 --> 00:23:05,900
The broad light-blue row is one HBB transcript from GENCODE.

224
00:23:05,900 --> 00:23:16,375
It describes a messenger RNA, and the browser has drawn it where its exons fall in genomic DNA — three blocks joined by lines, the blocks exons, the lines introns.

225
00:23:16,375 --> 00:23:28,950
It is drawn prominently because it belongs to MANE Select: a set of representative transcripts where RefSeq and Ensembl agree, chosen so everyone describing a gene can describe the same transcript.

226
00:23:28,950 --> 00:23:31,600
That is all the prominence means.

227
00:23:31,600 --> 00:23:36,050
It says nothing about how much of this transcript the cell actually makes.

228
00:23:36,650 --> 00:23:45,500
The large red feature beside it is a different kind of thing — a candidate transcript that GENCODE labels "to be experimentally confirmed".

229
00:23:45,500 --> 00:23:47,650
Something was seen there.

230
00:23:47,650 --> 00:23:52,975
What it is has not been settled, and UCSC draws that category in red.

231
00:23:52,975 --> 00:23:55,525
Worth remembering in general.

232
00:23:55,525 --> 00:23:59,425
A feature being in a database does not make it established.

233
00:23:59,425 --> 00:24:05,225
Read what the annotation claims, and what supports it, before you build anything on top of it.

234
00:24:05,825 --> 00:24:11,400
The green row is a different gene altogether, one that makes a long non-coding RNA.

235
00:24:11,400 --> 00:24:15,900
It overlaps the HBB region but sits on the opposite strand.

236
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:22,775
The green block is a single exon, and the long line running right leads to the next one, outside this window.

237
00:24:22,775 --> 00:24:24,900
There is no protein.

238
00:24:24,900 --> 00:24:32,525
This is why context matters: the same stretch of chromosome carries annotations for more than one gene, on both strands.

239
00:24:32,525 --> 00:24:38,950
But overlapping HBB does not by itself mean this RNA has anything to do with HBB.

240
00:24:39,550 --> 00:24:43,525
The compressed dark-blue rows are more HBB transcripts.

241
00:24:43,525 --> 00:24:51,550
Compare them and the differences show up — exon boundaries that do not line up, transcripts starting or ending in different places.

242
00:24:51,550 --> 00:24:58,525
Some of those differences are in untranslated regions only, which means the protein can come out identical.

243
00:24:58,525 --> 00:25:01,725
The rows are drawn short so more of them fit.

244
00:25:01,725 --> 00:25:06,800
The height says nothing about abundance, and nothing about the size of the molecule.

245
00:25:06,800 --> 00:25:12,125
One gene, several transcript records, each describing a particular RNA.

